We present Topological Relational System Dynamics (TRSD), a unified theoretical
framework deriving modified gravitational dynamics, a dark matter condensate, and
dark energy from a single path integral over a spacetime metric and a complex scalar
field. Beginning from a single variational principle with Einstein--Hilbert gravity,
a bilinear scalar kinetic term with coefficient $\delta c^4/(16\pi G) = \fdelta^2/2$,
and the stable dark-sector potential encoded directly in the axiom,
$V(z) = \frac{1}{2}(z-2)^2 + 2$,
we derive the complete dynamical system in a sequence of exact steps with no free
parameters beyond the Hubble horizon scale.

The MOND-like acceleration scale
$a_0 = (\sqrt{3}/\pi^2)\,cH_0$
is derived from the first nonzero eigenvalue of the discrete Laplacian on the
minimal closed $C_3$ cycle of the cosmological horizon. For
$H_0 = 73.04\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}\,Mpc^{-1}}$ this yields
$a_0 = 1.2452\times 10^{-10}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$.
The interpolation exponent $n = \pi/3$ is fixed by the total conical deficit and
confirmed against the SPARC dataset at the $0.27\%$ level.
Solar system screening, derived from the same deficit angle $\beta = \pi/2$, suppresses
any anomalous Mercury acceleration to $\delta g \sim 10^{-31}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$,
sixteen orders of magnitude below the INPOP19a ephemeris bound.

The dark-sector stable vacuum is encoded directly in the axiom through the
potential $V(z) = \frac{1}{2}(z-2)^2 + 2$, providing dark energy with equation of
