\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
% =====================================================================

The observed flatness of spiral galaxy rotation curves
\citep{Rubin1980} and the tightness of the radial acceleration relation
(RAR) \citep{McGaugh2016} present a double challenge to modern cosmology.
Within the $\Lambda$CDM paradigm, both phenomena require a dark matter halo whose
distribution must conspire to trace the baryonic content with remarkable precision.
In Modified Newtonian Dynamics (MOND) \citep{Milgrom1983a,Milgrom1983b,Milgrom1983c},
both phenomena follow from a single empirical postulate: below an acceleration scale
$a_0 \simeq 1.2\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, the effective gravitational
acceleration satisfies $g \to \sqrt{a_0 g_{\rm bar}}$, where $g_{\rm bar}$ is the
Newtonian acceleration from the baryonic mass alone.

Despite four decades of observational confirmation \citep{FamaeyMcGaugh2012,Lelli2017},
MOND and its Lagrangian formulation AQUAL \citep{BekensteinMilgrom1984} treat $a_0$
as a free parameter and leave unexplained its coincidence with the product $cH_0$ to
within a factor of order unity. Relativistic extensions, including TeVeS
\citep{Bekenstein2004}, COVARIANT MOND \citep{Hossenfelder2020}, and bimetric
MOND \citep{Milgrom2009}, introduce additional fields and degrees of freedom without
resolving the origin of $a_0$.

Meanwhile, ultralight (fuzzy) dark matter \citep{Hu2000} has emerged as a compelling
candidate that suppresses small-scale structure through quantum pressure while
maintaining $\Lambda$CDM behavior on large scales. Solitonic cores in dwarf galaxy
halos \citep{Schive2014} are a distinctive prediction with mass $m \sim 10^{-22}\,\mathrm{eV}$.
No existing theory unifies the MOND phenomenology with a quantum coherent dark matter
sector from the same fundamental field.

Topological Relational System Dynamics (TRSD) resolves both problems simultaneously.
Starting from a single variational principle, the framework derives:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item An effective interpolation function $\mueff(x)$ from a three-dimensional
    angular average over a threshold distribution on the cosmic horizon.
  \item The MOND acceleration scale $a_0 = (\sqrt{3}/\pi^2)\,cH_0$ from the first
    eigenvalue of the $C_3$ cycle Laplacian â with no free parameters.
  \item A screened high-acceleration regime passing all solar system tests.
  \item A dark matter condensate and dark energy from the stable scalar potential encoded in the axiom.
  \item The exact cosmic energy budget $\Omega_b \approx 4.99\%$,
    $\Omega_c \approx 27.15\%$, $\Omega_\Lambda \approx 67.87\%$ from two derived
    ratios: $\Omega_\Lambda/\Omega_c = C_3/C_2 = 5/2$ and
    $\Omega_b/\Omega_c = \alpha_{\rm TRSD}\,n = \sqrt{3}/(3\pi)$.
\end{enumerate}

