\section{The Coil Generator}
\label{sec:coil}
% =====================================================================

The cosmic horizon is generated by a coil: a pair of conjugate helical
lobes, one right-handed and one left-handed, meeting at a waist. The
envelope of the coil is a bicone. The swept volume is a sphere. The
$C/S$ shape is the lobe of the coil seen at $45^\circ$.

\subsection{Parametrization}

A single lobe is parametrized by
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{r}_+(t) =
  \begin{pmatrix}
    R_0 \sin t \cos(\omega t) \\
    R_0 \sin t \sin(\omega t) \\
    p\,t
  \end{pmatrix},
  \qquad t \in [0, \pi],
  \label{eq:coil_lobe_plus}
\end{equation}
where $R_0$ is the maximum radius, $\omega$ the twist rate, and $p$ the pitch.
The radius $R(t) = R_0 \sin t$ vanishes at $t = 0$ and $t = \pi$, so the lobe
pinches to a point at both ends. The conjugate lobe is the mirror image,
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{r}_-(t) =
  \begin{pmatrix}
    R_0 \sin t \cos(-\omega t) \\
    R_0 \sin t \sin(-\omega t) \\
    -p\,t
  \end{pmatrix},
  \qquad t \in [0, \pi].
  \label{eq:coil_lobe_minus}
\end{equation}
The two lobes are conjugate in both twist ($\omega \to -\omega$) and height
($p \to -p$). They share the pinch point at $t = 0$.

\subsection{Three views}

The coil presents three silhouettes depending on the viewing angle. All three
describe the same object.

\textbf{Side view.} The projection
$(x, z) = (R_0 \sin t \cos(\omega t),\; p t)$ traces the envelope of a bicone.
For large twist, the envelope shows the quasar-like morphology: a central pinch
point and two collimated lobes.

\textbf{Diagonal view ($45^\circ$).} The projection reveals the $S$ shape:
one lobe spiraling in one sense, the conjugate lobe in the opposite sense.
This is the $C/S$ shape of the original construction.

\textbf{Axial view.} The projection is a circle with a central point and two
spiral arms, one spiraling toward the observer and one away.

The $C$ is the lobe of the coil seen from the appropriate angle. It is still
a $C$. It has torsion.

\subsection{The bicone as envelope}

The envelope of the coil is a bicone with half-angle $\alpha$ fixed by the
ratio of pitch to radius:
\begin{equation}
  \tan\alpha = \frac{R_0}{p}.
  \label{eq:bicone_angle}
\end{equation}
The bicone is the light-like causal boundary. The $C_3$ triangle lives on the
bicone. The discrete Laplacian acts on the bicone nodes.

\subsection{Three nodes from the crossings}

The two conjugate lobes intersect where their positions coincide:
\begin{equation}
  \cos(\omega t) = \cos(-\omega t)
  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
  \sin(\omega t) = 0
  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
  \omega t_n = n\pi, \quad n \in \mathbb{Z}.
\end{equation}
Within one lobe, $t \in [0, \pi]$, distinct crossings occur at $n = 0, 1, 2$.
The first crossing is the pinch point at the waist. The second and third are
the outer nodes. The three nodes are equally spaced in azimuth by $2\pi/3$,
reproducing the $C_3$ symmetry.

\subsection{Spin axes}

The two lobes carry independent spin. One lobe spins about the vertical
axis; the conjugate lobe spins about the horizontal axis. The combined
motion sweeps the full sphere and generates the three-dimensional
structure.

