\section{Axiom and Variational Principle}
\label{sec:axiom}
% =====================================================================

% The path-integral form is replaced by the classical variational principle.
% H_0, a_c, a_0, and R_a relations come from the stationary cosmological solution
% and the C_3 area condition (unchanged from prior derivation).

\noindent\textbf{Geometric origin.}
The cosmic horizon is not a static sphere. It is generated by two conjugate
C-shaped arcs that spin about perpendicular axes. A threefold wobble in this
two-C/S geometry creates three conical defects at equal azimuthal separations,
each with deficit angle $\pi/3$. The total deficit is $\delta_{\rm total}=\pi$,
giving an effective horizon area $2\pi R_H^2$ and boundary circumference $2\pi R_H$.
The three defects close into the $C_3$ cycle. This is a structural description of
the geometry; the variational axiom encodes it through the kinetic coefficient.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Geometric derivation of the threefold count.}
The threefold count is the unique outcome of the two-C/S
geometry.  The argument is the spherical analogue of the Lissajous construction:
two equal-frequency perpendicular oscillators with a $90^\circ$ phase offset ---
precisely the configuration of two C-arcs counter-rotating about perpendicular axes
--- determine a unique closed nodal figure on $S^2$.  For generators of semicircular
span ($\pi$~rad each), that figure is a $C_3$ trefoil.  Its three nodes, at equal
$2\pi/3$ azimuthal separations, are the positions where the combined counter-rotation
yields zero net tangential displacement and horizon curvature therefore concentrates.
The defect closure condition (Section~\ref{sec:mass_defect}) partitions the total
deficit $\delta_{\rm total} = \pi$ equally among the three nodes, fixing
$\delta_j = \pi/3$, and the three-node skeleton closes into the $C_3$ minimal cycle.
The complete logical chain --- from the two-arc structure to the $C_3$ symmetry ---
is a single derivation, not two parallel descriptions:
\begin{equation}
  \text{two C-arcs on } {\perp} \text{ axes}
  \;\xrightarrow{\;\text{Lissajous}\;}
  3\text{ nodes at } 2\pi/3
  \;\xrightarrow{\;\text{closure}\;}
  \delta_j = \tfrac{\pi}{3}
  \;\xrightarrow{\;\text{skeleton}\;}
  C_3.
  \label{eq:lissajous_chain}
\end{equation}
The $C_3$ symmetry is a property of the two-C/S shape itself.
It \emph{is} that shape, expressed at the level of the horizon boundary.

The TRSD framework is defined by the variational principle
\begin{equation}
  \delta\!\int d^4x\,\sqrt{-g}\left\{
    \frac{c^4}{16\pi G}\,R
    -\frac{\delta c^4}{16\pi G}\,g^{\mu\nu}\partial_\mu\phi_+\partial_\nu\phi_-
    + 2\phi_+\phi_-
    - \tfrac{1}{2}(\phi_+\phi_-)^2
    - 4
  \right\} = 0,
  \label{eq:action}
\end{equation}
where $\delta$ is the conical deficit angle. The kinetic coefficient satisfies the
\emph{defect identification}
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{\frac{\delta c^4}{16\pi G} = \frac{\fdelta^2}{2},}
  \label{eq:kinetic_id}
\end{equation}
which follows from $\fdelta^2 = \delta c^4/(8\pi G)$ (derived in
Section~\ref{sec:defect_map}). This is a notation change; the kinetic coefficient
is the same object $\fdelta^2/2$ as in the normalized-field form.

The Lagrangian density for the scalar sector is
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\phi = -\frac{\fdelta^2}{2}\,g^{\mu\nu}\partial_\mu\phi_+\partial_\nu\phi_-
    + 2\phi_+\phi_- - \tfrac{1}{2}(\phi_+\phi_-)^2 - 4.
  \label{eq:scalar_lag}
\end{equation}
The potential energy (defined so that $\mathcal{L}_\phi^{\rm kin} - V$) is
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{V(\Xii) = \tfrac{1}{2}\Xii^2 - 2\Xii + 4 = \tfrac{1}{2}(\Xii-2)^2 + 2,}
  \label{eq:potential}
\end{equation}
where the scalar invariant is
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{\Xii \;\equiv\; \phi_+\phi_-.}
  \label{eq:Xi_def}
\end{equation}
This potential is a perfect square plus 2. It is \emph{globally stable}, with a unique
minimum at $\Xii = 2$ and $V_{\min} = 2$. No inversion step is required. The constant
$-4$ in $\mathcal{L}_\phi$ achieves the same result as the prior two-step procedure
(unstable potential $\to$ inversion) in a single axiom.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Field structure.}
The fields $\phi_\pm$ are the chiral components of a single complex scalar
$\Phi = \phi\,e^{i\theta}$, with $\phi_+ = \phi\,e^{i\theta}$ and
$\phi_- = \phi\,e^{-i\theta}$. This eliminates ghost degrees of freedom.
The invariant $\Xii = \phi_+\phi_- = \phi^2$ is a scalar-field product and
is \emph{not} interpreted as a particle mass. In polar form the kinetic term
becomes
\begin{equation}
  g^{\mu\nu}\partial_\mu\phi_+\partial_\nu\phi_-
  = \partial_\mu\phi\,\partial^\mu\phi
    + \phi^2\,\partial_\mu\theta\,\partial^\mu\theta,
  \label{eq:polar_kinetic}
\end{equation}
confirming two physical degrees of freedom: a radial mode $\phi$ and a phase
$\theta$, both real.

