\subsection{Variation with Respect to \texorpdfstring{$\phi_+$}{phi+}}

Setting $\delta S/\delta\phi_+ = 0$ and integrating by parts.
The potential contribution is $\partial P/\partial\phi_+ = (2-\Xii)\phi_-$
where $P = 2\phi_+\phi_- - \frac{1}{2}(\phi_+\phi_-)^2 - 4$:
\begin{equation}
  \frac{\fdelta^2}{2}\,\square\phi_- + (2 - \Xii)\,\phi_- = 0.
  \label{eq:variation_phi_plus_raw}
\end{equation}
Dividing by $\fdelta^2/2$:
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{\square\phi_- + \frac{2}{\fdelta^2}(2 - \Xii)\,\phi_- = 0.}
  \label{eq:eom_phi_minus}
\end{equation}
Expanding: $\square\phi_- + \frac{4}{\fdelta^2}\phi_- - \frac{2}{\fdelta^2}\phi_+\phi_-^2 = 0$.
The mass-like term $+4\phi_-/\fdelta^2$ is now \emph{positive}, consistent with
stability. At the vacuum $\Xii = 2$ the equation gives $\square\phi_- = 0$ (massless
Goldstone) and the restoring force points toward the vacuum for all $\Xii \neq 2$.

\subsection{Variation with Respect to \texorpdfstring{$\phi_-$}{phi-}}

By exchange symmetry $\phi_+ \leftrightarrow \phi_-$:
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{\square\phi_+ + \frac{2}{\fdelta^2}(2 - \Xii)\,\phi_+ = 0.}
  \label{eq:eom_phi_plus}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Normalized Field Equations}

Using $\phi_\pm = \fdelta\,\varphi_\pm$ and $\Xii = \fdelta^2\varphi_+\varphi_-$,
the equations~\eqref{eq:eom_phi_minus}--\eqref{eq:eom_phi_plus} become
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{\square\varphi_\pm + \frac{2}{\fdelta^2}(2 - \fdelta^2\varphi_+\varphi_-)\,\varphi_\pm = 0.}
  \label{eq:eom_normalized}
\end{equation}
This system is second-order in each field and coupled nonlinearly through
$\Xii = \fdelta^2\varphi_+\varphi_-$. The positive mass-like coefficient
$+4\varphi_\pm/\fdelta^2$ at $\Xii=0$ reflects the stable potential.
This nonlinearity is the origin of the modified gravitational dynamics at low accelerations.

\subsection{Stress-Energy Tensor}

The stress-energy tensor of the scalar sector is
