Consider the vacuum partition function over $k$ unit-vector degrees of freedom
$\mathbf{u}_j$, $|\mathbf{u}_j|=1$:
\begin{equation}
  Z_v(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{j=1}^{k} e^{\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{u}_j},\qquad
  W_v(\mathbf{x}) = \ln Z_v(\mathbf{x}).
  \label{eq:vacuum_partition}
\end{equation}
This is the cumulant generating function for the microscopic defect directions.
Computing the gradient at $\mathbf{x} = 0$, where $Z_v(0)=k$:
\begin{equation}
  \frac{\partial W_v}{\partial x_a}(0)
  = \frac{1}{k}\sum_j u_{j,a} = 0,
  \label{eq:vacuum_stationarity}
\end{equation}
since the vacuum is stationary (isotropic vector set: $\sum_j \mathbf{u}_j = 0$).

The Hessian at $\mathbf{x}=0$ is
\begin{equation}
  T_{ab}(0) = \frac{1}{k}\sum_j u_{j,a}u_{j,b}.
  \label{eq:hessian_zero}
\end{equation}
Lorentz invariance (no preferred direction) requires $T_{ab} = C\,\delta_{ab}$.
Taking the trace and using $|\mathbf{u}_j|^2 = 1$:
\begin{equation}
  \sum_a T_{aa} = 3C = \frac{1}{k}\sum_j |\mathbf{u}_j|^2 = 1
  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad C = \tfrac{1}{3}.
  \label{eq:isotropy_trace}
\end{equation}
Therefore
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{T_{ab}(0) = \tfrac{1}{3}\,\delta_{ab}.}
  \label{eq:isotropy}
\end{equation}
The Taylor expansion of $W_v$ is
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{W_v(\mathbf{x}) = W_v(0) + \frac{|\mathbf{x}|^2}{6} + \mathcal{O}(|\mathbf{x}|^3).}
  \label{eq:Wv_expansion}
\end{equation}
The coefficient $1/3$ arising from isotropy is not a free parameter. It propagates
through the angular reduction (Section~\ref{sec:angular}) to fix the $3/4$ coefficient
