\section{Minimal Closed Cycle \texorpdfstring{$C_3$}{C3} and Critical Acceleration}
\label{sec:C3}
% =====================================================================

A conical defect \emph{is} a cone. The minimal discrete closed cycle that reproduces
the topology of a cone â three edges meeting at a point, closing back on itself â is
the triangle: three nodes, three arcs, cycle $C_3$. No smaller closed cycle exists on
the horizon that can close a conical deficit; $C_2$ is degenerate (a line), and any
$C_n$ with $n \geq 4$ is not minimal. $C_3$ is therefore \emph{forced} by the
geometry of the light-like conical defect
itself. This is the discrete skeleton of the future light cone at the horizon boundary.

The cosmic horizon has radius $R_H = c/H_0$ and circumference $L_H = 2\pi R_H$.
The minimal closed cycle $C_3$ divides the horizon into three equal arcs:
\begin{equation}
  \ell = \frac{L_H}{3} = \frac{2\pi R_H}{3}.
  \label{eq:segment}
\end{equation}
The discrete Laplacian on $C_3$ acts on the Fourier basis $f_n = e^{2\pi i m n/3}$
(nodes $n = 0,1,2$; mode $m$) as
\begin{equation}
  (\Delta f)_n = \frac{1}{\ell^2}\left(2f_n - f_{n+1} - f_{n-1}\right).
  \label{eq:discrete_laplacian}
\end{equation}
The eigenvalue for mode $m$ is
\begin{equation}
  \lambda_m = \frac{2}{\ell^2}\left[1 - \cos\!\left(\tfrac{2\pi m}{3}\right)\right].
  \label{eq:eigenvalue}
\end{equation}
For $m=1$, $\cos(2\pi/3) = -1/2$, so $\lambda_1 = 3/\ell^2$. Substituting
$\ell^2 = 4\pi^2 R_H^2/9$:
\begin{equation}
  \lambda_1 = \frac{27}{4\pi^2 R_H^2}.
  \label{eq:lambda1}
\end{equation}
For lightlike propagation along the horizon, the characteristic frequency is
$\omega_1 = c\sqrt{\lambda_1} = 3\sqrt{3}\,c/(2\pi R_H)$,
and the period is
$\tau_1 = 2\pi/\omega_1 = 4\pi^2 R_H/(3\sqrt{3}\,c)$.
The critical acceleration is \emph{defined} as $\ac = c/\tau_1$.  Physically,
this is the centripetal acceleration experienced by a photon that traverses one
complete $C_3$ cycle â three horizon arcs each of length $\ell$ â in time $\tau_1$
at the speed of light.  It is the unique dimensionally correct acceleration
constructible from $c$ and $\tau_1$ alone, and is a physically motivated
definition rather than a theorem.  Substituting:
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{\ac = \frac{3\sqrt{3}}{4\pi^2}\,cH_0.}
  \label{eq:ac}
\end{equation}
This derivation is entirely parameter-free: $\ac$ is determined solely by horizon
topology and the Hubble expansion rate. For $H_0 = 73.04\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}\,Mpc^{-1}}$:
\begin{equation}
  \ac = 9.339\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}.
  \label{eq:ac_numerical}
\end{equation}

