\subsection{Curvature Integral and the Defect--Mass Relation}
\label{subsec:gauss_bonnet}

For a disk $D$ carrying a conical singularity of deficit $\delta$, the
two-dimensional Gauss--Bonnet theorem gives
\begin{equation}
  \int_D R\sqrt{g}\,d^2x = 2\pi - 2\pi\Bigl(1-\frac{\delta}{2\pi}\Bigr)
  = \delta .
  \label{eq:gauss_bonnet}
\end{equation}
The defect is light-like and therefore extends in time. Integrating the
spacetime curvature over the $2{+}1$ dimensional region swept out in proper
time $\tau$,
\begin{equation}
  \int_\tau\!\!\int_D R^{(3)}\sqrt{g^{(3)}}\,d^2x\,dt = \delta\tau ,
  \label{eq:gauss_bonnet_3d}
\end{equation}
and equating the Einstein--Hilbert energy $c^4\delta\tau/(8\pi G)$ to the rest
energy $Mc^2\tau$ swept over the same interval gives
\begin{equation}
  \frac{c^4\delta\tau}{8\pi G} = Mc^2\tau
  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
  \delta = \frac{8\pi GM}{c^2 L_{\rm ref}} .
  \label{eq:delta_mass}
\end{equation}
The source is a point mass at the apex, so $GM/c^2$ is a length and
$L_{\rm ref}$ is the length of the embedding. By
equation~\eqref{eq:unit_basis} that length is $R_H$, giving the dimensionless
\begin{equation}
  \delta = \frac{8\pi GM}{c^2 R_H} .
  \label{eq:delta_mass_RH}
\end{equation}
Equivalently $M$ enters as the energy per unit length
$T = Mc^2/R_H$. The reference length cancels in
$\delta c^4/(8\pi G) = Mc^2/R_H = McH_0$, so every downstream quantity is
independent of it.

