\section{Cascade Structure and the Closure Weights}
\label{sec:cascade}
% =====================================================================

The quantized sequences of Section~\ref{subsec:tor_sequences} are the first
members of a discrete ladder generated by the two-C/S construction. This
section derives the ladder and the closure weights that fix the wobble term of
equation~\eqref{eq:wobble} and the Sachs--Wolfe plateau form.

\subsection{Arc--sphere cardinality}

Two perpendicular arcs enclose exactly one region. A chain of $N_a$ arcs
contains $N_a-1$ adjacent arc-pairs, each enclosing one sphere, so
\begin{equation}
  N_a = N_s + 1 .
  \label{eq:arc_sphere}
\end{equation}
The base $C_3$ chain of Section~\ref{sec:C3} has $(N_a,N_s) = (3,2)$.

\subsection{Two ladders}

The base $C_3$ has threefold rotational symmetry. Adding one arc breaks it;
adding two, one per adjacent node-pair, preserves it. The
\emph{symmetry-preserving} ladder therefore advances in steps of
$\Delta N_a = 2$,
\begin{equation}
  N_a = 2j+1 = 3,5,7,\dots, \qquad N_s = 2j,
  \label{eq:sym_ladder}
\end{equation}
and controls the interpolation exponent. The \emph{full} ladder runs over every
arc--sphere pair permitted by~\eqref{eq:arc_sphere},
\begin{equation}
  (N_a,N_s) = (k+2,\;k+1), \qquad k = 1,2,3,\dots,
  \label{eq:full_ladder}
\end{equation}
and controls the closure weights. The symmetric ladder is the odd-$k$
sub-ladder of the full one. Below, $k$ always refers
to~\eqref{eq:full_ladder}.

\subsection{Interpolation exponent}

On the symmetric ladder the exponent factorizes into a closure fraction
$R_j = N_a/N_s = (2j+1)/(2j)$ and a scale reduction. Each level adds two arcs,
each contributing a factor $2$ in configuration space, hence $2^2 = 4$ per
level; normalizing $S_1 = 1$ gives $S_j = 4^{-(j-1)}$. Therefore
\begin{equation}
  n_j = \frac{2j+1}{2j}\,2^{-2(j-1)} = \frac{N_a}{N_s}\,2^{-(N_a-3)} ,
  \label{eq:cascade_exponent}
\end{equation}
taking the values $1.500000$, $0.312500$, $0.072917$, $0.017578$, $0.004297$
for $j = 1,\dots,5$.

\subsection{Closure weights and their sum}

On the full ladder define the closure weight as the reciprocal of the
arc--sphere product,
\begin{equation}
  \kappa_k = \frac{1}{N_aN_s} = \frac{1}{(k+1)(k+2)}
  = \tfrac16,\ \tfrac1{12},\ \tfrac1{20},\ \tfrac1{30},\ \tfrac1{42},\dots
  \label{eq:kappa}
\end{equation}
This measures the fraction of structure at level $k$ that has not yet closed
into the next ring. Partial fractions give
$\kappa_k = (k+1)^{-1} - (k+2)^{-1}$, so the partial sum telescopes,
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{k=1}^{K}\kappa_k = \frac12 - \frac{1}{K+2}
  \;\longrightarrow\;
  \sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\kappa_k = \frac12,
  \qquad
  \sum_{k=2}^{\infty}\kappa_k = \frac12-\frac16 = \frac13 .
  \label{eq:telescope}
\end{equation}
The two sums are the framework's polar pair, beginning at $(N_a,N_s) = (3,2)$
and $(4,3)$ respectively; their sum is $5/6$, their difference $1/6 = \kappa_1$,
and their ratio $3/2$, the base closure fraction. The total,
$\sum_k\kappa_k = 1/2$, is the displacement of the closure ratio from its base
value $3/2$ to its limit $1$, and coincides with the conformal gluing factor of
Section~\ref{sec:complete_ending}.

The weight at $k=2$ is $\kappa_2 = 1/12$, which is the additive wobble term of
equation~\eqref{eq:a0_trsd_cand}.

\subsection{Sachs--Wolfe plateau}

For a scale-invariant primordial spectrum the plateau has the form
$C_\ell \propto 1/[\ell(\ell+1)]$. Identifying the multipole with the sphere
count, $\ell = N_s = k+1$,
\begin{equation}
  C_\ell \propto \frac{1}{(k+1)(k+2)} = \kappa_k .
  \label{eq:sw_cascade}
\end{equation}
The correspondence is structural rather than numerical: \eqref{eq:arc_sphere}
forces consecutive integers into the denominator of~\eqref{eq:kappa}, and
consecutive integers $\ell(\ell+1)$ are exactly the Laplacian eigenvalues on
the sphere. Summing, $\sum_{\ell\ge2}C_\ell \propto 1/2$.

This reproduces the plateau \emph{form}. Acoustic peak positions and
amplitudes require transfer functions the framework does not supply.

\subsection{Recursive depth}

Every node of a $C_3$ is itself a $C_3$, so the structure at depth $d$ contains
\begin{equation}
  N_d = \sum_{j=0}^{d}3^{j} = \frac{3^{d+1}-1}{2}
  = 1,\ 4,\ 13,\ 40,\ 121,\dots
  \label{eq:recursive_depth}
\end{equation}
units. No axiom selects a depth, so the recursion is unbounded, and the minimal
decision unit consistent with the framework is ternary.

\subsection{Space as arc-excess}

At every level $N_a - N_s = 1$. The excess arc is the boundary generator of the
enclosed region: without it the sphere is a static region admitting no
distinction between interior and exterior; with it the level carries a
preferred direction, the boundary normal, along which the ladder advances. The
unit excess is the cascade's directional degree of freedom, and dark energy is
the wobble-weighted integral of that excess over all levels.

