\section{Screening Exponent and Screened Response}
\label{sec:screening}
% =====================================================================

\subsection{Geometric Origin of the Screening Exponent}

The total conical deficit $\delta_{\rm total} = \pi$ modifies scalar field propagation
in the eikonal limit. A mode traversing a closed loop that encircles the conical
defect acquires a path-dependent phase. For a conical geometry with deficit angle
$\delta$, the phase accumulated on such a loop is $e^{-i\delta/2}$, a standard result
of wave propagation on cone-like spacetimes \citep{VilenkinShellard1994}.

The deviation of the gravitational response from its Newtonian value, $1 - \mueff(x)$,
is sourced by this phase mismatch. In the high-acceleration regime the field completes
many oscillations per scale length, so the suppression of the non-Newtonian tail is
controlled by the accumulated phase factor. Translating the phase to an amplitude
suppression in terms of the dimensionless acceleration $x = g/\ac$ gives a factor
$(1+x)^{-\delta/2}$ multiplying $1-\mueff(x)$.

For $\delta_{\rm total} = \pi$ the screening exponent is
\begin{equation}
  \beta = \frac{\delta_{\rm total}}{2} = \frac{\pi}{2},
  \label{eq:screening_exponent}
\end{equation}
and the screened effective response is
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{\muscr(x) = 1 - \frac{1-\mueff(x)}{(1+x)^{\pi/2}}.}
  \label{eq:mu_scr}
\end{equation}
The exponent $\beta = \pi/2$ is a direct geometric consequence of the total conical
deficit, and follows from the defect geometry.

\subsection{Limiting Behavior and Solar System Bound}

In the low-acceleration regime ($x\to 0$), $(1+x)^{\pi/2}\to 1$, so
$\muscr \approx \mueff$ and MOND dynamics are fully recovered. In the
high-acceleration (Newtonian) regime ($x\to\infty$), using
$1-\mueff(x) \sim 3/(2x)$ from equation~\eqref{eq:mueff}:
\begin{equation}
  1 - \muscr(x) \sim \frac{3}{2}\,x^{-(1+\pi/2)}.
  \label{eq:screening_decay}
\end{equation}
The deviation from Newtonian gravity decays as $x^{-(1+\pi/2)} \approx x^{-2.571}$,
which is sixteen orders of magnitude faster than the unscreened deviation at
solar-system accelerations. For Mercury at $x_\odot \approx 2\times10^{10}$:
\begin{equation}
  \delta g_{\rm Mercury}
  \approx \frac{3}{2}\,\frac{\ac}{x_\odot^{1+\pi/2}}
  \sim 10^{-31}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}},
  \label{eq:mercury_bound}
\end{equation}
compared to the INPOP19a ephemeris bound of $10^{-15}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$
\citep{Fienga2019}: sixteen orders of magnitude of headroom, with no free parameters.

