\subsection{High-Occupation Limit}

The quantum field operator for the radial mode $\chi$ is
\begin{equation}
  \hat{\chi}(t,\mathbf{x}) = \int\frac{d^3k}{(2\pi)^3}\,\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\omega_k}}\!
  \left[\hat{a}_k\,e^{-i\omega_k t + i\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{x}}
  + \hat{a}_k^\dagger\,e^{+i\omega_k t - i\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{x}}\right],
  \label{eq:quantum_field}
\end{equation}
with $[\hat{a}_k, \hat{a}_{k'}^\dagger] = (2\pi)^3\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf{k}-\mathbf{k}')$.
For macroscopic occupation number $N \gg 1$, the coherent-state approximation replaces
the operator by its expectation value: $\langle\hat{\chi}\rangle = \chi_{\rm cl}$.
This is the controlled large-$N$ limit of the QFT.

\subsection{Nonrelativistic Envelope}

The mean field is decomposed as
\begin{equation}
  \chi_{\rm cl}(t,\mathbf{x}) = \frac{\hbar}{\sqrt{2m_\delta}}\!
  \left[\psi(t,\mathbf{x})\,e^{-im_\delta c^2 t/\hbar}
  + \psi^*(t,\mathbf{x})\,e^{+im_\delta c^2 t/\hbar}\right],
  \label{eq:nr_envelope}
\end{equation}
where $\psi(t,\mathbf{x})$ varies slowly on timescales $\sim\hbar/(m_\delta c^2)$.

\subsection{Gross-Pitaevskii Equation}

In the nonrelativistic limit $|\partial_t\psi| \ll m_\delta c^2|\psi|/\hbar$, the
equation of motion for $\chi_{\rm cl}$ reduces to the Gross-Pitaevskii (GP) equation:
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{i\hbar\,\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial t}
    = -\frac{\hbar^2}{2m_\delta}\nabla^2\psi
    + m_\delta\Phi\,\psi
    + \frac{3\hbar^3}{2m_\delta^2\,c}\,|\psi|^2\psi.}
  \label{eq:gpe}
\end{equation}
The three terms represent: quantum pressure (gradient energy), gravitational potential
energy, and self-interaction from the quartic coupling $\lambda_\chi = 12$.
The local mass density is $\rho_\chi = m_\delta|\psi|^2$.

\subsection{Quantum Pressure}

From the gradient term in equation~\eqref{eq:gpe}, the quantum pressure is
\begin{equation}
  Q = -\frac{\hbar^2}{2m_\delta^2}\,\frac{\nabla^2\!\sqrt{\rho_\chi}}{\sqrt{\rho_\chi}}.
  \label{eq:quantum_pressure}
\end{equation}
This provides the support against gravitational collapse below the de Broglie scale,
eliminating cusps in dark matter density profiles.

