potential at the last scattering surface. The Sachs--Wolfe effect relates this
potential to the temperature anisotropy:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\Delta T}{T}(\hat n) = -\frac{1}{3}\Phi(\hat n).
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Geometry of the Three Defects}

Place the three generator defects on the celestial sphere at polar angle
$\theta=\pi/2$ (the equatorial plane) and azimuthal angles
\begin{equation}
\phi_j = \frac{2\pi(j-1)}{3}, \qquad j=1,2,3.
\end{equation}
This configuration realises the $C_3$ symmetry of the horizon.

\subsubsection{Spherical Harmonic Expansion of the Potential}

The gravitational potential of a single conical defect with deficit angle
$\delta_j$ at position $\hat n_j$ is proportional to a delta function on the
sphere:
\begin{equation}
\Phi_j(\hat n) \propto \delta_j\,\delta^{(2)}(\hat n - \hat n_j).
\end{equation}
The total potential is the sum of three contributions:
\begin{equation}
\Phi(\hat n) = A\sum_{j=1}^{3}\delta_j\,\delta^{(2)}(\hat n - \hat n_j),
\end{equation}
where $A$ is an amplitude determined by the horizon tension.

Expanding in spherical harmonics,
\begin{equation}
\Phi(\hat n) = \sum_{\ell=0}^{\infty}\sum_{m=-\ell}^{\ell}
b_{\ell m} Y_{\ell m}(\hat n),
\end{equation}
we obtain
\begin{equation}
b_{\ell m} = A\sum_{j=1}^{3}\delta_j\, Y_{\ell m}^*(\hat n_j).
\end{equation}
Using $\delta_j = \pi/3$ and the explicit positions, the azimuthal sum is
\begin{equation}
\sum_{j=1}^{3} e^{-im\phi_j} =
1 + e^{-2\pi i m/3} + e^{-4\pi i m/3},
\end{equation}
which is non-zero only when $m$ is an integer multiple of $3$:
\begin{equation}
\sum_{j=1}^{3} e^{-im\phi_j} =
\begin{cases}
3, & m = 0, \pm 3, \pm 6, \ldots, \\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Therefore the conical horizon potential contains only spherical harmonics with
$m = 3k$, $k\in\mathbb{Z}$.

The configuration carries a second symmetry. All three defects lie in the plane
orthogonal to the cone axis, so it is invariant under the reflection
$\hat z\mapsto-\hat z$. Under $\theta\mapsto\pi-\theta$ one has
$Y_{\ell m}(\pi-\theta,\phi) = (-1)^{\ell+m}Y_{\ell m}(\theta,\phi)$, so
invariance requires $\bigl((-1)^{\ell+m}-1\bigr)b_{\ell m} = 0$. The complete
selection rule is therefore
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{b_{\ell m} = 0 \quad\text{unless}\quad
  m \equiv 0\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 3)
  \ \ \text{and}\ \ \ell+m\ \text{even}.}
  \label{eq:selection_rule}
\end{equation}
Both conditions are needed. The rule admits $b_{20}$ ($3\mid0$, $\ell+m=2$
even) and $b_{3,\pm3}$ ($3\mid3$, $\ell+m=6$ even), and forbids $b_{22}$ and
$b_{30}$ --- exactly the pattern used below to obtain $C_3/C_2 = 5/2$.

\subsubsection{Leading Harmonics: Quadrupole and Octupole}

