This forces all modes to propagate at the speed of light, $v^2 = c^2$. No
additional force is required; the boundary condition selects the null regime.
The horizon does not act as a dynamical force but as a topological constraint
that enforces masslessness at the terminal state.

\subsection{Penrose-Style Conformal Cyclic Mapping}
\label{subsec:conformal_cyclic_final}

The conformal boundary of the terminal universe is identified with the
conformal boundary of the initial Big Bang through a conformal rescaling.
Following \cite{Penrose2010}, the infinite future of one aeon is glued to the
infinite past of the next by the conformal factor $\Omega \to 0$ at both
boundaries. The physical metrics of the two aeons are related by
\begin{equation}
\tilde{g}_{\mu\nu}^{\text{(next)}} = \Omega^2\, g_{\mu\nu}^{\text{(old)}},
\label{eq:conformal_gluing_final}
\end{equation}
where $\Omega$ is chosen to map the future boundary to the past boundary.
This gluing is smooth and preserves the conformal structure. The two boundaries
are not merely similar; they are the same conformal hypersurface viewed from
different aeons.

\subsection{Complete Causal Chain}
\label{subsec:complete_chain_final}

The complete causal chain from the terminal state to the new Big Bang is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Terminal conformal boundary: massless, scale-free, with action
      \eqref{eq:boundary_action_final}.
\item Quantum trace anomaly \eqref{eq:trace_anomaly_final} generates a
