\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Finiteness and the Landscape of All Possible Universes}
\label{subsec:finiteness_final}

A natural extension of the cyclic framework is to ask how many distinct
universes the cycle can produce. The terminal conformal boundary is not
featureless: it carries residual quantum fluctuations and a quantized horizon
area. After the conical defect, the effective horizon area is
\begin{equation}
A_{\rm eff} = 2\pi R_H^2,
\end{equation}
where $R_H$ is the horizon radius. The smallest area unit is the Planck area
\begin{equation}
\ell_P^2 = \frac{\hbar G}{c^3}.
\end{equation}
The total number of Planck cells on the defect-modified horizon is therefore
\begin{equation}
N = \frac{A_{\rm eff}}{\ell_P^2}
= \frac{2\pi R_H^2}{\ell_P^2}
\approx 4.0 \times 10^{122}.
\end{equation}
Each Planck cell encodes one qubit of information, so the number of distinct
boundary configurations is
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\mathcal{N}_{\rm universes} = 2^{N}
= 2^{\,4.0 \times 10^{122}}.
}
\end{equation}
This finite but astronomically large number is the total possible number of
distinct universes that the cycle can produce. It is not infinite, but for all
practical purposes it replaces infinity: no physical process can enumerate or
explore even a tiny fraction of these possibilities within a single aeon.

Similarly, quantum fluctuations prevent the energy density at the conformal
boundary from ever being exactly zero. The boundary is a limit, not an actual
state of nothingness. Thus zero is also eliminated from physical ontology:
there is a smallest nonzero fluctuation scale, set by the trace anomaly and
the finite horizon area. The framework therefore predicts that physics is
strictly finite, bounded from below by quantum uncertainty and from above by
the horizon area quantization.

This completes the picture: the universe is not an infinite multiverse, nor a
singular one-off, but a finite cyclic system whose total number of possible
configurations is given by the defect-quantized horizon area. All physical
quantities lie strictly between zero and this finite maximum. The TRSD
framework thus provides a concrete, quantitative version of the Finiteness
Principle.

