\section{Derivation of the Isotropic Angular Average}
\label{app:angular}

The integral $I = \int_0^1 u^3(1+xu)^{-1}\,du$ is evaluated by polynomial long division:
\[
  \frac{u^3}{1+xu} = \frac{u^2}{x} - \frac{u}{x^2} + \frac{1}{x^3}
  - \frac{1}{x^3(1+xu)}.
\]
Integrating each term from $0$ to $1$:
\begin{align*}
  \int_0^1 \frac{u^2}{x}\,du &= \frac{1}{3x},\quad
  \int_0^1 \frac{u}{x^2}\,du = \frac{1}{2x^2},\quad
  \int_0^1 \frac{du}{x^3} = \frac{1}{x^3},\quad
  \int_0^1 \frac{du}{x^3(1+xu)} = \frac{\ln(1+x)}{x^4}.
\end{align*}
Therefore $I = 1/(3x) - 1/(2x^2) + 1/x^3 - \ln(1+x)/x^4$, and
$\sigma(x) = 3x^2 I$ yields equation~\eqref{eq:sigma}.

\section{Stability of the Dark Sector Vacuum}
\label{app:dark_stability}

The second derivative of $V_{\rm dark}(z) = -2z + \frac{1}{2}z^2 + 4$ with respect to $\phi$,
at $z = \fdelta^2\phi^2$:
\[
  \frac{dV_{\rm dark}}{d\phi} = V_{\rm dark}'(z)\cdot 2\fdelta^2\phi
  = (-2 + z)\cdot 2\fdelta^2\phi.
\]
Setting to zero: $\phi = 0$ or $z = 2$, i.e., $\phi_0 = \sqrt{2}/\fdelta$.
\[
  \frac{d^2 V_{\rm dark}}{d\phi^2}\bigg|_{\phi_0}
  = V_{\rm dark}''(z)\cdot 4\fdelta^4\phi_0^2 + V_{\rm dark}'(z)\bigg|_{z=2}\cdot 2\fdelta^2
  = 1\cdot 4\fdelta^4\cdot\frac{2}{\fdelta^2} + 0 = 8\fdelta^2 > 0.
\]
The vacuum is stable for all $\fdelta > 0$.


